  document.querySelector('#research-state').insertAdjacentHTML('afterend',`<section class="valuation-boundary" aria-labelledby="valuation-boundary-heading"><p class="kicker">How to read the numbers</p><h2 id="valuation-boundary-heading">Different businesses need different cash measures</h2><p class="valuation-boundary-intro">The archive does not force every business into one cash measure. Each screen states what is being valued, what remains a claim ahead of shareholders, and which assumptions still need evidence.</p><div class="valuation-boundary-grid"><article><p class="state-status">Common screen</p><h3>224 source-linked rows</h3><p>Most companies use a cautious estimate of cash left for shareholders after operating costs, investment, acquisitions, stock compensation, and other stated claims.</p></article><article><p class="state-status">Different measure</p><h3>Affirm and Annaly</h3><p>Affirm uses credit-adjusted operating earnings. Annaly uses book value and earnings available for distribution. Neither is comparable to ordinary operating cash.</p></article><article><p class="state-status">Reader rule</p><h3>Not a price target</h3><p>Bear, base, and bull cases show what the current valuation requires. They are assumptions to test against future filings, not predictions.</p></article></div><p>${link('Read the valuation coverage map','notes/valuation-coverage-audit-2026-09-14.md')}</p></section>`);
